# air-cargo

## Project Overview
Air cargo flight tracking and trade flow inference system.
Polls ADS-B data from OpenSky Network to track cargo aircraft,
detect anomalous behaviour, and infer freight trade flows.
Companion learning project — builds Flink, SedonaFlink, 
and Kafka skills before returning to maritime AIS work.

## Repository Structure
air-cargo/
├── adsb-collector/    OpenSky REST polling → Kafka
├── adsb-analyser/     Flink streaming + batch, detection + inference
├── adsb-tools/        Standalone diagnostic and export utilities
├── common/            Shared models, constants, enums
├── reference-data/    Airport, operator reference data scripts
│   └── coverage/      Hand-drawn coverage polygon layers (GeoJSON)
└── k8s/               K3s deployment manifests

## Language and Build
- Java 17
- Maven, no parent pom — each module independent
- Do not suggest Gradle, Kotlin, or Spring Boot
- Do not fetch external URLs autonomously
- If documentation needed, ask user to provide the section

## Core Technology Stack
- Apache Flink — streaming and batch processing (no Spark)
- Apache SedonaFlink — spatial operations on Flink streams
- Redpanda — Kafka-compatible broker (refer to as Kafka in code)
- PostgreSQL + PostGIS — spatial reference layers + JSONB state
- Delta Lake — permanent analytical archive, completed flights
                and gap records beyond Kafka retention window
- DuckDB — ad-hoc analytical queries on Delta Lake tables,
           has spatial extension for ST_ operations
- OkHttp — HTTP client for OpenSky polling
- Jackson — JSON serialisation
- Protobuf — internal Kafka event topics only

## Infrastructure
Primary development machine: ThinkPad (Linux)
  Redpanda, PostGIS (Docker), IntelliJ Community
Secondary machine: used for code-only work without Kafka/PostGIS
Single node deployment target: Minisforum CachyOS
No cloud dependency. No always-on requirement.
Collector runs whenever machine is on.
Quality of results proportional to uptime — accepted limitation.

## Java 17 / Flink Runtime
Flink's Kryo serialiser requires --add-opens flags under Java 17.
These must be set in:
  IntelliJ Run Configuration → VM options:
    --add-opens java.base/java.util=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.lang.reflect=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.io=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.net=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.nio=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/sun.nio.ch=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.util.concurrent=ALL-UNNAMED
    --add-opens java.base/java.util.concurrent.atomic=ALL-UNNAMED
  Also set in maven-surefire-plugin argLine for tests.
Jackson must be pinned to 2.14.2 to match Flink 1.19's
shaded Jackson — do not upgrade.

## OpenSky Network API
Base URL: https://opensky-network.org/api
Authentication: OAuth2 client credentials
  - client_id and client_secret from environment variables
    OPENSKY_CLIENT_ID and OPENSKY_CLIENT_SECRET
  - Bearer token, expires every 30 minutes
  - Refresh proactively at 25 minutes

Key endpoints:
  GET /states/all?extended=1
    - current state vectors
    - up to 1hr past with time parameter (authenticated)
    - optional bounding box: lamin, lomin, lamax, lomax
    - extended=1 adds category field (index 17)

  GET /flights/aircraft?icao24=X&begin=T1&end=T2
    - flights for specific aircraft, max 2 day window
    - used for watchlisted aircraft catch-up on restart
    - expensive in credits — use sparingly

Polling strategy — two tier adaptive:
  Tier 1 discovery: global /states/all every 10-15 minutes
    ~400 credits/day, finds new interesting aircraft
  Tier 2 watchlist: higher frequency for flagged aircraft
    specific ICAO24 queries, ~3,600 credits/day remaining
    tightens on events: tanker airborne, unusual route,
    aircraft disappears, aircraft reappears
  Collector reads watchlist dynamically from PostgreSQL
  and adjusts query parameters accordingly.

Token refresh: proactively before 30 minute expiry (at 25 min)
Kafka broker: from env var KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS,
              default localhost:9092

## State Vector Field Indices
OpenSky returns states as arrays — deserialise by index:
  0:  icao24          hex string, unique transponder address
  1:  callsign        8 chars, nullable, trim whitespace
  2:  origin_country  inferred from icao24
  3:  time_position   unix timestamp last position, nullable
  4:  last_contact    unix timestamp last contact
  5:  longitude       WGS-84 decimal degrees, nullable
  6:  latitude        WGS-84 decimal degrees, nullable
  7:  baro_altitude   metres, nullable
  8:  on_ground       boolean — unreliable, use as confirming
                      signal only, not primary signal
  9:  velocity        m/s over ground, nullable
  10: true_track      degrees clockwise from north, nullable
  11: vertical_rate   m/s positive=climbing, nullable
  12: sensors         receiver IDs, nullable, skip
  13: geo_altitude    metres, nullable
  14: squawk          transponder code, nullable
  15: spi             special purpose indicator boolean
  16: position_source 0=ADS-B 1=ASTERIX 2=MLAT 3=FLARM
  17: category        only present when extended=1,
                      0=no information (very common)

## on_ground Unreliability
on_ground = true is rare and unreliable in OpenSky data.
Ground-level ADS-B reception is poor. Do not rely solely
on on_ground flag for landing/takeoff detection.
Use altitude + speed + position heuristics as primary signals:

Landing detection:
  vertical_rate strongly negative
  + velocity decreasing toward ~250 km/h
  + baro_altitude approaching known field elevation
  + position near known airport (ST_Within)
  + track aligned with runway centreline
  → flight phase: ON_APPROACH → LANDED

Takeoff detection:
  velocity increasing
  + vertical_rate strongly positive
  + altitude increasing
  + position near known airport

## Flight Phase State Machine (per aircraft, Flink keyed state)
UNKNOWN
  → position received, on_ground=false → AIRBORNE
  → position received, on_ground=true → ON_GROUND

AIRBORNE
  → position, on_ground=false → extend track
  → descent profile + airport proximity → ON_APPROACH
  → no message > threshold → LOST_TRACKING (do not close flight)

ON_APPROACH
  → on_ground=true OR disappears during approach → LANDED
  → altitude increases again → AIRBORNE (go-around)

LANDED / ON_GROUND
  → velocity increases + climbing → new Flight, AIRBORNE
  → if takeoff position ≠ landing airport → flag anomaly

LOST_TRACKING
  → reappears on_ground=false → gap segment, resume, AIRBORNE
  → reappears on_ground=true → landed during gap, ON_GROUND

## Flight and Track Data Model
Flight:
  flightId          icao24 + takeoff_timestamp (composite key)
  icao24            permanent aircraft identifier
  callsign          nullable, may change between flights
  takeoffTime       Instant
  takeoffAirport    enriched from PostGIS airports layer
  landingTime       Instant, null if still airborne
  landingAirport    enriched from PostGIS airports layer
  status            AIRBORNE | LANDED | LOST_TRACKING
  track             List<TrackPoint>
  gaps              List<GapRecord>

TrackPoint:
  timestamp         Instant
  latitude          Double
  longitude         Double
  baro_altitude_m   Double  (used for flight phase detection)
  geo_altitude_m    Double  (GPS-derived, preferred for spatial ops)
  velocity          Double
  heading           Double
  positionSource    Integer (0=ADS-B preferred)
  — interpolated field removed, see Track Point Tiers below

## Track Point Tiers
Three tiers of points — must never be mixed in analytical data:

  Tier 1: OBSERVED
    Real ADS-B data points from OpenSky
    Have a real timestamp and full position fields
    Full analytical value
    Stored in Delta Lake tracks table

  Tier 2: INFERRED
    Positions analytically deduced (e.g. gap classification)
    Have analytical meaning but are not observations
    Carry uncertainty / confidence score
    Stored in Delta Lake gaps table with explicit tier label

  Tier 3: TESSELLATION
    Purely geometric nodes for great circle rendering
    NO analytical meaning whatsoever
    Never stored in tracks or gaps tables
    Stored only in implied_tracks display table
    Generated by a separate tool from gap endpoints
    Never joined into analytical queries

## Gap Data Model (Tier 1 facts only)
GapRecord — stored in Delta Lake gaps table:
  gap_id                  uuid
  flight_id               links to flights table
  icao24
  last_observed_lat       } Tier 1 — real observed position X
  last_observed_lon       }
  last_observed_ms        }
  last_observed_alt_m     }
  last_observed_heading   }
  first_reobserved_lat    } Tier 1 — real observed position Y
  first_reobserved_lon    }
  first_reobserved_ms     }
  first_reobserved_alt_m  }
  polls_missed            integer — observed fact:
                          "we looked N times and saw nothing"
  gap_duration_ms         = first_reobserved_ms - last_observed_ms
  implied_speed_ms        ST_DistanceSphere(X,Y) / gap_duration_ms
  physically_plausible    implied_speed_ms < max_aircraft_speed
  gap_classification      OCEAN | COVERAGE_HOLE |
                          ANOMALOUS | UNCLASSIFIED
  classification_confidence 0.0-1.0

implied_tracks — display-only table, separate from gaps:
  gap_id                  FK to gaps table (one-way reference)
  geom                    LineString, tessellated great circle
                          from last_observed to first_reobserved
  display_only            always true — never use in analysis
  Generated by ImpliedTrackGenerator batch job on demand.
  When coverage polygons are corrected: truncate gaps
  classifications and implied_tracks, rerun batch job.

## Coverage Classification
ADS-B coverage gaps are not equal. Classification depends on
hand-drawn coverage polygon layer (reference-data/coverage/).
Polygons drawn by hand in QGIS, stored as GeoJSON.

Gap classification logic:
  last_observed_position within NORMAL_COVERAGE polygon
  AND gap is unexplained
  → ANOMALOUS (high anomaly score — transponder off?)

  gap crosses open ocean (no coverage possible)
  AND implied_speed plausible
  → OCEAN (flight continued, draw implied track)

  gap within known structural coverage hole
  (China, Central Asia, parts of Russia, Central Africa)
  AND short duration → probably continued through
  AND long duration  → may have landed, undetectable
  → COVERAGE_HOLE

Coverage polygons are not permanent truth — they are a prior.
If ANOMALOUS gaps cluster near a polygon boundary, the polygon
may be drawn too large. Review flagged gaps in QGIS and correct
polygon. After correction: truncate classifications and rerun
GapClassifier batch job. Full reclassification is cheap at
current data volumes — no partial reprocessing needed.

Note: zero density in a grid cell ≠ coverage hole.
Could also mean no aircraft fly there (off-corridor, 
uninhabited). Coverage polygons are drawn from visual
inspection of position density + domain knowledge,
not purely from zero-density cells.

## Streaming vs Batch Boundary
Kafka / Flink streaming (continuous, per-record):
  Raw detection → flight phase → position enrichment
  Everything that is per-record and low-latency
  Output: adsb.tracks, adsb.plane_state (Kafka)
          completed flights → Delta Lake (on landing)
          detected gaps → Delta Lake (on gap close)

Flink batch (periodic, triggered, set-based):
  GapClassifier job:
    reads gaps from Delta Lake
    joins with coverage polygons (PostGIS or file)
    classifies each gap
    merges classification back to Delta Lake

  ImpliedTrackGenerator job:
    reads CONTINUED / OCEAN gaps from Delta Lake
    generates tessellated great circle geometries
    writes to Delta Lake implied_tracks table (display only)
    uses PostGIS ST_Segmentize on geography type:
      ST_Segmentize(
        ST_MakeLine(X, Y)::geography,
        100000  -- node every 100km
      )::geometry

  (future) TradeFlowInference job:
    reads completed flights from Delta Lake
    spatial + temporal reasoning across datasets
    writes trade_flow inferences

Flink batch uses RuntimeExecutionMode.BATCH on same
StreamExecutionEnvironment — no Spark, no separate cluster.

## Delta Lake Architecture
Permanent analytical archive — data that outlives Kafka's
30-day retention window and requires cross-record queries.

Tables:
  flights         one row per completed flight
                  written by Flink streaming on landing
                  updated via MERGE as flight record evolves
                  partitioned by date

  tracks          enriched positions, OBSERVED tier only
                  partitioned by date + icao24
                  Z-ordered by (latitude, longitude) for
                  efficient spatial queries

  gaps            one row per detected gap, Tier 1 facts only
                  gap_classification populated by batch job
                  MERGE used for classification updates

  implied_tracks  tessellated great circle display geometries
                  display_only=true, never joined analytically
                  truncated and regenerated after polygon edits

MERGE pattern (not plain append):
  Flink emits completed/updated flight records
  Delta Lake MERGE: match on flight_id
    WHEN MATCHED → UPDATE changed fields
    WHEN NOT MATCHED → INSERT
  Allows anomaly score updates, landing enrichment,
  gap classification updates without rewriting records.

OPTIMIZE schedule (periodic maintenance):
  OPTIMIZE tracks ZORDER BY (latitude, longitude)
  OPTIMIZE flights ZORDER BY (landing_lat, landing_lon)
  Z-ordering co-locates spatially nearby records in same
  Parquet files — DuckDB spatial queries skip more data.

DuckDB for ad-hoc queries:
  Reads Delta Lake tables directly via delta_scan()
  Spatial extension supports ST_ operations
  Geometry stored as separate lat/lon double columns —
  DuckDB constructs geometry at query time:
    ST_Point(longitude, latitude)
  No WKB geometry column needed at current scale.

## Kafka Topics
  adsb.states       JSON, raw OpenSky snapshots
                    one message per poll, key=snapshot timestamp
                    30 days retention, delete policy
                    written by adsb-collector
                    never transcode or reprocess raw content

  adsb.tracks       Protobuf (PositionUpdate), enriched positions
                    one message per aircraft per poll
                    keyed by icao24
                    30 days retention, delete policy
                    written by adsb-analyser (Flink)
                    includes: flight_id, flight_phase,
                    baro_altitude_m, geo_altitude_m, anomaly_score

  adsb.plane_state  Protobuf (PlaneState), current state per aircraft
                    log compacted — latest value per ICAO24 key
                    written by adsb-analyser (Flink)
                    frontend and diagnostic tools subscribe here

  adsb.flights topic was considered and dropped — lifecycle
  events are reconstructable from adsb.tracks phase transitions.
  Completed flight records written to Delta Lake instead.

## Serialisation
- adsb.states: raw JSON string — never transcode in collector
- adsb.tracks: Protobuf (PositionUpdate)
- adsb.plane_state: Protobuf (PlaneState)
- Delta Lake: Parquet columnar, lat/lon as double columns
- Document state: PostgreSQL JSONB
- Internal topics always Protobuf, external/raw always JSON

## Protobuf Messages (adsb-analyser/src/main/proto/aircraft.proto)
All messages in package io.aircargo.analyser,
java_package io.aircargo.analyser.proto

PositionUpdate: enriched position → adsb.tracks
  icao24, flight_id, timestamp_ms
  latitude, longitude
  baro_altitude_m (used for flight phase detection)
  geo_altitude_m  (GPS-derived, preferred for spatial ops)
  velocity_ms, heading_deg, vertical_rate_ms
  position_source (PositionSource enum)
  flight_phase (FlightPhase enum)
  interpolated, anomaly_score

PlaneState: current state per aircraft → adsb.plane_state
  icao24, aircraft_type, operator
  flight_phase, timestamp_ms
  latitude, longitude, altitude_m, velocity_ms, heading_deg
  current_flight_id, departed_from, departed_at_ms
  anomaly_score

Enums:
  FlightPhase: FLIGHT_PHASE_UNKNOWN, AIRBORNE, ON_APPROACH,
               ON_GROUND, LOST_TRACKING
  PositionSource: POSITION_SOURCE_UNKNOWN, ADS_B, MLAT,
                  ASTERIX, FLARM

## Key Identifiers
ICAO24: permanent aircraft identifier, primary key throughout
        hex string, always lowercase
Callsign: changes per flight, not reliable as identifier
          trim whitespace — OpenSky pads to 8 chars
Registration: tail number, links to external registries
FlightId: icao24 + takeoff_timestamp_ms — unique per flight
GapId:    uuid, generated on gap detection

## System-Generated Identifiers
FlightId, gap_id, journey_id, anomaly_score, flight_phase —
all generated by our Flink pipeline. Not sourced from external data.
FlightId = icao24 + takeoff_timestamp_ms

## Aircraft Categories (extended=1)
  0: No information (very common — most aircraft)
  4: Large (75,000-300,000 lbs)
  5: High Vortex Large (B-757 class)
  6: Heavy (> 300,000 lbs)
Category field unreliable for filtering — use ICAO24
registry lookup for aircraft type identification instead.

## Aircraft Metadata Reference Table (PostgreSQL)
Maps icao24 → type, registration, operator, manufacturer.
Write-once, rarely-update (physical airframe never changes).
Sources in order:
  1. FAA registry CSV (free bulk download, daily updated)
  2. OpenSky aircraft database CSV (free download)
  3. planespotters.net API (free tier, gaps only,
     cache result permanently — never re-query)
Reload FAA + OpenSky CSVs weekly via script in reference-data/.
Zero OpenSky credits consumed for enrichment.

## Aircraft Types of Interest
Boeing 747F / 747-8F    large intercontinental cargo
Boeing 777F             large, increasingly common
Boeing 767F             medium, FedEx/UPS workhorse
Airbus A330F            medium cargo
Ilyushin IL-76          Soviet-era, common Africa/Middle East
                        often associated with grey market ops
Antonov AN-124          enormous, military/oversized cargo
                        unusual routes always notable
KC-135 / KC-46          US military tankers
                        orbit patterns imply nearby operations
C-17 Globemaster        US military transport
A400M                   European military transport

## Operators of Interest
Integrators (baseline behaviour, not primary focus):
  FedEx, UPS Airlines, DHL Aviation

Charter/ad-hoc (anomaly targets):
  Atlas Air, Silk Way Airlines, Air Bridge Cargo
  Volga-Dnepr, smaller post-Soviet operators

Military (tracking interest):
  US Air Mobility Command (RCH*, REACH* callsigns)
  NATO logistics aircraft

## Sedona Operations Planned
1. Airport proximity:
   ST_Within(aircraft_position, airport_boundary)
   + altitude check vs field elevation
   → gap classification, landing detection

2. Runway alignment (approach detection):
   Bearing calculation vs runway centreline geometry
   → ON_APPROACH state confirmation

3. Gap implied speed:
   ST_DistanceSphere(last_known, reappearance) / gap_duration
   → physical plausibility check

4. Tanker range cone:
   ST_Buffer(last_known_position, max_range_km)
   intersected with heading sector
   → post-disappearance uncertainty cone

5. Airport comparison:
   ST_Distance(takeoff_airport, reappearance_position)
   → same airport vs repositioning vs anomaly

6. Route deviation (future):
   ST_HausdorffDistance(observed_track, baseline_track)
   → anomaly scoring

7. Sensitive location proximity (future):
   ST_Within(position, sensitive_area_polygon)
   → flag cargo aircraft near military/sanctioned locations

## PostGIS Schema
Database: postgres (local Docker, postgis/postgis:16-3.4)
Connection: localhost:5432, user=postgres, pass=postgres
Schemas:
  reference   static reference data loaded from CSVs
  flights     completed flight records (future)
  analytics   materialized views, aggregations (future)

reference schema tables:
  airports    ~47,000 rows (large/medium/small only)
              typed columns + JSONB properties
              geom: geometry(Point, 4326) with GIST index
              loaded from OurAirports airports.csv
  runways     ~35,000 rows
              linked to airports via airport_ident FK
              includes both runway end headings (le/he)
              loaded from OurAirports runways.csv

PostGIS Schema Style:
  Hybrid: typed columns for core fields,
  JSONB properties column for flexible attributes.
  GIN index on properties for efficient JSONB queries.
  Special location attributes (mro, boneyard, grey_market_hub)
  stored in airports.properties JSONB column.

Airport proximity lookup:
  AirportLookup class in adsb-analyser
  JDBC connection opened in Flink open(), closed in close()
  ST_DWithin with geography cast, threshold 5000m
  Called on takeoff and landing detection
  Returns airport ident or null if none within threshold
  Failures logged as warnings, never fail the Flink job

## Two-Tier State Management
Hot tier (Flink RocksDB):
  Active flights and recent track history
  TTL 48-72 hours no contact
  Watchlisted aircraft: TTL suppressed

Cold tier (Delta Lake):
  Completed flights, gaps, long-term history
  On landing or TTL expiry: write to Delta Lake
  On reappearance: check Delta Lake for previous flight context

## PostgreSQL JSONB Collections (operational only)
  aircraft        per-aircraft metadata and state
  watchlist       tracked aircraft + cone state
  Completed flights and gaps → Delta Lake, not PostgreSQL JSONB

## Spatial Operations
- ST_DistanceSphere for distance calculations
- Coordinates WGS-84 (EPSG:4326) throughout
- OpenSky returns longitude (index 5) then latitude (index 6)
  be explicit when constructing geometry points
- Altitude always check alongside 2D spatial queries

## Flink Pipeline (adsb-analyser)
Single Flink job, AdsbAnalyserMain:

  KafkaSource(adsb.states)
    → flatMap SnapshotSplitter: explode → AircraftPosition
    → keyBy(icao24)
    → FlightStateMachine (KeyedProcessFunction)
        main output:  PositionUpdate → adsb.tracks
        side output:  PlaneState → adsb.plane_state
        maintains FlightState per aircraft in RocksDB
        calls AirportLookup on takeoff/landing detection
        on landing: writes completed flight → Delta Lake
        on gap close: writes GapRecord → Delta Lake

Consumer group: adsb-analyser (bump version suffix on
  deliberate full replay: adsb-analyser-v2, v3 etc.)
Starting offsets: OffsetsInitializer.earliest()

## Anomaly Scoring
Fuzzy 0.0-1.0 confidence scores throughout.
No binary thresholds. Scores accumulate in per-aircraft
Flink state. Alert only when score sustained above threshold
over time window. Currently placeholder (always 0.0) —
not yet implemented.

## Catch-up on Restart
On adsb-collector startup:
  Query /states/all with time=now-3600 for 1hr recovery
  For each watchlisted ICAO24: query /flights/aircraft
  for past 48 hours to recover missed flights

## adsb-tools Module
Standalone diagnostic and export utilities, no Flink.
Uses plain kafka-clients KafkaConsumer.
Always use assign() + seekToBeginning() not subscribe()
to avoid partition assignment race condition on small topics.

PlaneStateExporter:
  reads adsb.plane_state → output/plane_state.geojson
  one point per aircraft, properties include flight_phase

TracksExporter:
  reads adsb.tracks → output/tracks.geojson
  groups PositionUpdate records by flight_id
  sorts by timestamp_ms ascending within each flight
  one LineString feature per flight_id
  skips flight_ids with fewer than 2 positions
  properties: flight_id, icao24, first_seen_ms,
              last_seen_ms, point_count, flight_phase

## QGIS Layer Styling
Recommended layer combination for pipeline debugging:
  plane_state.geojson — point layer
    categorise by flight_phase for colour coding
  tracks.geojson — line layer
    direction arrow at last vertex shows heading:
      Layer Properties → Symbology
      Symbol type: Line
      Add symbol layer → Marker line
      Marker placement: Last vertex only
      Marker shape: Arrow or Filled triangle
      Enable: Rotate marker to follow line direction

## Design Decisions
- Flink not Spark — streaming and batch, one stack, one cluster
- Raw JSON preserved in adsb.states — never transcode
- Delta Lake not PostgreSQL JSONB for completed analytical records
- DuckDB for ad-hoc Delta Lake queries — no query engine needed
- JSONB in PostgreSQL for operational state only — no MongoDB
- Independent Maven modules — no parent pom
- Observational language — never judgemental
- on_ground unreliable — use heuristics
- Category field unreliable — use ICAO24 registry lookup
- 30 day Kafka retention — critical for development replay
- adsb.flights topic dropped — not needed
- Track point tiers strictly separated — tessellation never
  stored in analytical tables, never mixed with observed data
- Coverage polygons hand-drawn, not algorithmically derived —
  zero density ≠ coverage hole (could be off-corridor)
- Gap reclassification is full truncate + rerun, not partial —
  cheap at current data volumes, avoids version complexity

